export const GUIDE_TASKS: GuideTask[] = [
  { title: "Find what uses the most energy", steps: ["Open Facility → Devices.", "“What stands out” names the biggest single user and what it cost.", "“Where the energy goes” ranks every device with its share and cost. Click a name to open that device."] },
  { title: "See what is left on after working hours", steps: ["Open Analysis and go to step 2, “What stays on after working hours” (on a site with lights-on hours, “What stays on in the daytime”).", "Or open Facility → Devices and look at the heatmap: red or orange cells under “After working hours” mean the device is running while the site is closed.", "Items marked “Check first” may need to run all the time, such as fridges or servers. Lights ticked Always on by design are tagged “Always on by design” and are never offered as a saving."] },
  { title: "See which lights were on in the daytime", steps: ["For a site with lights-on hours. When daytime use reaches 10% of the total, the Overview lists On in the daytime: each light, the hours it was on, on how many days, and its kWh.", "The same list is in the site report and the Management review, and Analysis step 2, “What stays on in the daytime”, opens with it.", "A light that comes on or goes off within 20 minutes of the lights-on hours counts as on time and is not listed. Lights ticked Always on by design are left out and named on their own."] },
  { title: "Look at one board or one device", steps: ["Open Facility → Devices.", "Under Show, pick a board (for example DB2) to see everything on that board only.", "Or choose Open a device and search for it. Use Previous and Next to step through devices.", "Each view has its own link, so you can copy the address and share it."] },
  { title: "Check a single day, such as a holiday", steps: ["Open Analysis, go to step 3 and choose Single day. Or, in step 4, choose See that day hour by hour beside a day that stood out.","Click the date to open the calendar. Weekends are shaded, holidays are orange and days with missing readings have a dashed border.", "The chart shows that day hour by hour, with working hours unshaded."] },
  { title: "Understand a peak", steps: ["Open Analysis and find Peak 1h Consumption in the Executive Summary.", "Choose View top 5 to see the busiest hours.", "The breakdown shows each space's share and, for the selected space, a chart of which circuits used the energy."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Update the electricity rate", steps: ["Open Facility → Electricity rate and choose Edit, or Add next rate in the warning.", "Enter the price per kWh and the dates exactly as they appear on your bill, and whether the price is before or including GST.", "Choose Save rate. Every cost figure in the app uses it straight away."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Change operating hours or add a holiday", steps: ["Operating hours: open Facility → Operating hours, choose Edit, set the hours for each day and save.", "Holidays: open Facility → Holidays, click a day or drag across several, choose the type and save.", "Saving updates reports and charts straight away."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Set lights-on hours for lighting that runs at night", steps: ["Open Facility → Operating hours and choose Edit.", "Under What the hours mean, choose Lights-on hours.", "Set the hours for every day. For hours past midnight, set an end earlier than the start, for example 19:00 to 07:00.", "Choose Save changes. Use outside these hours now counts as daytime use, and weekends, holidays and closures keep the same hours. A light that comes on or goes off within 20 minutes of these hours counts as on time.", "The Overview warns when daytime use reaches 10% of the total and lists the lights on in the daytime, with their hours. Tick Always on by design on emergency lights so they are left out."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Group meters and give them short names", steps: ["Open Facility → Floor layout. The Groups list is on the left.", "Choose + New group to add a group. Select a group to Rename it, add + Group inside (for example Block lighting and Emergency lights inside a block), move it with Move up or Move down, or Remove it once its meters have moved out.","Drag a meter onto a group, or choose Move to… beside the meter. The first time a meter moves, the name of its old group is kept as its Electrical panel. A new group goes live once it has a meter in it.", "For a shorter name, choose Edit on the meter and fill in Short name. It shows first everywhere, with the full name underneath. Where two meters would look the same, put the block in front, for example Block A Lift lobby.", "Totals and shares in reports, Devices and Analysis come from these groups, in the order set with Move up and Move down. The Overview shows each top-level group's kWh a day and share."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Mark emergency lights and record the number of lights", steps: ["Open Facility → Floor layout, select the meter's group and choose Edit beside the meter.", "Tick Always on by design for lights that must stay on day and night, such as emergency lighting. They stay in every total but are left out of daytime-use warnings and savings, and reports say running day and night is expected.", "Fill in Number of lights with how many light points the meter feeds, as a whole number. Leave it empty if you do not know.", "Choose Save. Reports then show Energy per light: kWh per light a day for each meter with a number, and for groups, counting only the meters that have one."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Arrange groups from a spreadsheet", steps: ["Open Facility → Floor layout and choose Arrange from a spreadsheet, below + New group.", "Choose an Excel (.xlsx) or CSV file with one row per meter. Download a template shows the columns: Group (use > for a group inside a group, for example Common area > Pavilion), Meter name and an optional Short name. The file stays on this computer.", "Check the columns, then the meters. Each row is matched to a meter: check any best guesses, and pick a meter or choose Skip this row for any that did not match.", "What will change lists the new groups, the meters that move and the short names. Groups left with no meters are removed unless you untick Remove them. No meters are removed.", "Optional: tick Also add a floor-plan area for each top-level group. Then choose Apply these groups. Nothing changes until you do."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Draw the floor plan and place devices", steps: ["Open Facility → Floor layout and choose Edit layout (on a new site, Draw the layout).", "Choose + Add area, + Add room or + Add equipment inside a room, then drag a box on the plan to draw it (a single click adds one at the usual size; Esc stops). Drag items to move them and pull a corner or the middle of an edge to resize. Pull any edge of the drawing space, left and top included, to make it bigger (when zoomed in, choose Fit plan first). + Add entrance shows where people come in.", "+ Text puts a label on the plan (a road name, a block number or a short note) and + Landmark adds a simple icon, such as a lift, stairs, toilets, a car park, a bin centre, a tree, a bus stop or a north arrow; click the plan where it goes. A new text label is ready to type into straight away. + Add entrance adds as many entrances as the site has; the first one is the main entrance. Select one to change its text or caption, size, colour or how far it is turned (turn by 15° with the buttons, type any angle, or drag the round handle above it); device boxes can be turned too. Text, landmarks and entrances are drawing only: they change no totals or groups.", "To work on several items at once, Shift-click them or drag a box across empty space (inside an area, hold Shift while dragging); they move, nudge and delete together. Duplicate (or ⌘/Ctrl+D) copies an area with its rooms and equipment, and ⌘/Ctrl+C then ⌘/Ctrl+V pastes copies; devices are never copied, and a copy has no panel until you pick one. Lock in place stops an item being moved or deleted by accident. Bring to front and Send to back choose which overlapping item is drawn on top.", "For exact placing, open Grid in the toolbar: turn snapping on or off, choose the grid spacing, or hide the grid. While snapping is on, a dragged item also lines up with the edges and centres of nearby items (a thin guide line shows it); hold ⌘/Ctrl while dragging to place it freely. With one item selected, type its X, Y, Width and Height under Position and size and press Enter. With several selected, Line up aligns their edges or centres, spaces them evenly, or gives them the same width or height. Colour has ten swatches or your own colour code, and for an area on a panel, Colour for everything on this panel colours that panel's areas, rooms and devices at once (unless one has its own colour).", "Choose “Place the rest in their board's area”: each device goes to the room its name mentions. Drag any device to its exact spot.", "Under Plan style, choose how the plan looks for everyone: Building drawing, Blueprint or Colour blocks; How it will look shows it as you go. Then choose Save layout. The plan updates straight away, including in Project notes and for the advisor.", "Undo and Redo fix mistakes, also with the keys right after clicking a button; for arrows and Delete, click the plan first. Holding an arrow down is one step to undo. Choosing Cancel or another Facility tab with changes not saved asks before leaving. Areas the saved layout holds without a place on the drawing are listed under Not on the drawing and kept as they are.", "To see more or less of the plan, use − and + in the toolbar, or hold ⌘/Ctrl and scroll, or pinch on a trackpad or touch screen. Fit plan shows the whole plan and 100% shows it at actual size; zooming never changes the plan itself. Zoomed in, scroll, hold Space and drag, or drag with two fingers to move around. On a touch screen, drag items with one finger, hold a finger on an item to add it to the selection, and to place a device tap Place and then tap the plan. Without a mouse, click the plan or Tab to it, press ] or [ to go through its items, Enter to select, the arrow keys to move and ⌘/Ctrl with an arrow key to resize. To place a device without a mouse, choose Place, go to a room with ] or [ and press Enter (Enter on the plan itself places it in the middle of what is shown). If the page closes before you save, the editor offers to restore your unsaved changes the next time you open it, signed in as you, in the same browser."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Set up a new site from data files", steps: ["Click the project name at the top left. Choose New client and enter the company's name (skip this if the client already exists).", "Choose New project, enter the site's name and timezone, then open the new project.", "Open Facility and choose Upload data. Drag the meter files (CSV or Excel exports from the meter app) into the box.", "Under “Check device names”, choose Add or change names: paste the device table from Excel, load a CSV, or choose Read from a photo of the table.", "Check the table, then choose Set up & publish. A distribution board, one group per device and the meter names are created, and the readings go live.", "Optional: set Operating hours and the Electricity rate so after-hours use and costs are worked out."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Add new data each month", steps: ["Export the latest readings from the meter app as CSV or Excel.", "Open Facility → Upload data and drag the file in. Each file shows which dates are new; dates already loaded are not counted twice.", "Choose Publish. “Published” shows the new date range, the readings added and any new devices.", "Keep device names the same in the meter app: a renamed device appears as a new one."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Set up a new site from Tuya (no file)", steps: ["Create the client and the site (New client, then New project), then open the new project.", "Open Facility and choose Live connection. Under Start from Tuya, enter the Tuya cloud project's Access ID and Access Secret and choose Connect. They are on platform.tuya.com: Cloud → Open Project → Overview tab → Authorization Key.", "The box at the top shows the connection and what Tuya holds: all devices, the ones that can be meters (online and offline), gateways, and how many are ticked. Refresh list asks Tuya again. One Tuya account can hold several sites' devices; a device another site already reads is marked Connected to another site and left unticked.", "With the site's own sheet (best): choose Paste your device list. In Excel, select from the heading row down to the last meter, copy, and paste. Headings such as Block Number, Meter Name, Meter Device ID and Meter Tracking Location are understood; a row with only a block (for example 264F) sets the block for the meters below it, and other columns, such as daily readings, are ignored. Or choose Load an Excel or CSV file and pick the sheet itself (.xlsx): a title row above the headings and notes below the meters are skipped.", "Choose Check the list. Each line shows Found, or why not (for example Not in this Tuya account: a wrong ID, or the app account that holds it is not linked to the cloud project). Fix those lines and check again.", "Choose Connect … meters and fetch the last 7 days. Only the meters on the sheet are connected. They get the sheet's names; a name used in more than one block gets the block in front (for example 264F Emergency Lighting). What each one tracks is kept, Floor layout groups the meters by block, the last 7 days come in, and the site then updates every day at 02:00.", "No sheet? Choose Tick devices from Tuya's list. Search by name, device type or online / offline, or paste device IDs (one or a whole column from Excel): the list shows just those devices, names any ID Tuya does not have, and Tick only these ticks exactly them. Untick anything that is not this site's meter, then choose Create … meters.", "Tuya keeps only about 7 days of readings, so connect a new site within a week of installing its meters. To draw the site afterwards, see Draw the floor plan and place devices."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Connect meters for live data (Tuya)", steps: ["For a site whose meters are already set up from data files or with the advisor. A new site with no meters can start from Tuya instead (see Set up a new site from Tuya).", "Open Facility and choose Live connection. Enter the Tuya cloud project's Access ID and Access Secret and choose Connect. The key is stored encrypted and never shown again.", "Match each meter to its Tuya device: choose Match by name, then pick any that are left from the list, and choose Save matches.", "Choose Check devices to confirm every device sends energy readings.", "Tick Update every day and pick the hour. Choose Fetch now to bring in the last week straight away.", "A site managed by EnergyX support (Tuya Office) shows its devices read-only. To edit it yourself, enter the same Access ID and Access Secret under Manage this site here instead and choose Manage here; Let support manage it hands it back.", "A meter that only has power while its lights are on (for example outdoor or linkway lights on a timer) goes offline every day while they are off. Open Facility → Floor layout, choose Edit on that meter and tick Turns off with its lights: it then shows Off with the lights instead of Device offline, and counts as offline, with an alert email, only after 16 hours."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Add new meters to a site on Tuya", steps: ["Open Facility → Live connection and choose Add new meters, in the step that matches each meter to its device.", "The list shows devices in the Tuya project that are not in this site yet. Tick the new ones, check each name and choose its group under Goes in. Tick Off with the lights for a meter that only has power while its lights are on.", "Choose Add … meters. This can take a minute. Readings arrive with the next daily update, or choose Fetch now."] },
  { adminOnly: true, title: "Add a client, or move or remove a project", steps: ["Click the project name at the top left to open the project list.", "New client adds a client. To move a project, drag it onto another client, or hover it and choose ⋯ → Move to another client.", "⋯ also offers Archive, which hides the project from the client but keeps its data, and Delete permanently, which needs the project's name typed to confirm."] },
  { title: "Create a report from the meter readings", steps: ["Open Reports and choose Create a report.", "Under From your meter readings, pick the dates (up to three months at a time).", "Choose a Style. Full report has every chart, table and check. Management review is short and plain, organised by group, with the things worth deciding. With groups, both set alike blocks side by side and show Energy per light where meters have a number of lights.", "Choose Create report. It is written in a few seconds, with no AI, and saved in Reports. Administrators can set the style of daily, weekly and monthly reports with Site report style in Automatic reports."] },
  { title: "Compare one block with another", steps: ["Name the blocks the same way, for example Block A and Block B, and give each the same groups inside, with the same names, for example Ground floor, Block lighting and Emergency lights.", "Create a report, or open the latest automatic one. A section such as Block A against Block B sets each group beside the same group in the other block, in kWh on an average day.", "The sentence above it names the biggest difference. Only groups found in each block are compared, matched by name."] },
  { title: "Get a report every day", steps: ["An administrator opens Reports → Automatic reports, chooses Daily or Daily + weekly + monthly under Frequency, sets the hour and the Site report style, then chooses Save schedule. The site needs a saved report method first (see Get a report or an answer).", "Each day around that hour, a site report for the day before is saved in Reports, written from the meter readings with no AI. The bell says when the newest one is ready.", "The Overview keeps showing the weekly or monthly report; daily ones stay in Reports. Under Length covered, choose 1 day to list only them."] },
  { title: "Get a report or an answer", steps: ["Open Energy advisor → Ask the advisor.", "Type your question in plain English and send it.", "Finished reports are kept in Reports.", "Reports every day, week or month (admins): open Reports → Automatic reports, choose how often, the hour and what each report should cover, then Save schedule. The site first needs a saved report method: after a report you like, choose Save report method and make it the site's default in Advisor guidelines."] },
];

export const GUIDE_CHARTS: GuideTerm[] = [
  { term: "Daily bars", meaning: "One bar per day. A grey strip under the axis marks a closed day. PH is a public holiday, PC a planned closure and SH a day with special hours." },
  { term: "Typical day", meaning: "Average power for each hour of the day. The shaded band shows operating hours, or lights-on hours. At a lights-on site with groups, the report draws one line per group." },
  { term: "Heatmap", meaning: "Each cell is one hour. The scale is “% of this device's busiest hour”: 100% is the busiest hour, as the note under the chart says, and 0% an hour with no use. Green is low, red is high. Hover over or tap a cell for its kW and kWh. The band above shows operating hours and after working hours." },
  { term: "Where it goes, by group", meaning: "Each top-level group's kWh on an average day and its share of all use, adding up to 100%, in the order set in Floor layout. Tap or click a group to open the groups inside it; their shares add up to the group's." },
  { term: "One block against another", meaning: "Alike blocks side by side, group for group, in kWh on an average day, with which uses more and by how much. Only groups found in each are compared, matched by name, such as Block lighting with Block lighting." },
  { term: "Every day, hour by hour", meaning: "On a single device, one row per day. All days use the same scale, so a busy day and a quiet day can be compared directly." },
  { term: "Floor plan", meaning: "Each colour is one distribution board. Pins are devices; the label leaves out the room name it sits in. Faded areas are not part of your selection." },
  { term: "Peak breakdown chart", meaning: "How one space's energy split between circuits in that hour. The grey “Not split by circuit” slice is energy the main meters recorded but no circuit meter explains." },
];

export const GUIDE_TERMS: GuideTerm[] = [
  { term: "kWh", meaning: "Energy used over time, and what the bill charges for. A 1 kW load running for one hour uses 1 kWh." },
  { term: "kW", meaning: "Power at a moment: how hard equipment is working right then." },
  { term: "Peak demand", meaning: "The highest 15-minute average power in the period." },
  { term: "Always-on load", meaning: "The power drawn in at least 9 of every 10 hours. It runs whether or not anyone is there. Devices also gives it per day, for example 0.1 kW ≈ 2.4 kWh a day." },
  { term: "After working hours", meaning: "Energy used outside the operating hours, including weekends, public holidays and planned closures." },
  { term: "Operating hours", meaning: "When the site is open, set in Facility → Operating hours." },
  { term: "Distribution board (DB)", meaning: "The electrical panel that feeds an area. Each board has its own meters." },
  { term: "Board total", meaning: "A meter that measures a whole board, such as all lighting on DB1. It overlaps the devices on that board, so it is not added to them." },
  { term: "No separate meter", meaning: "Energy a board recorded that no individual device meter explains. Extra metering there would show where it goes." },
  { term: "Readings received", meaning: "The share of expected readings that arrived. Below 95%, totals may be a little low." },
  { term: "Group", meaning: "Meters that serve the same thing, such as a block, a floor or a common area. Set in Facility → Floor layout. Totals and shares come from groups." },
  { term: "Short name", meaning: "A shorter name for a meter. It shows first everywhere, with the full name underneath." },
  { term: "Always on by design", meaning: "A tick on a meter's Edit form for lights that must run day and night, such as emergency lighting. They stay in every total but are left out of daytime use, its warnings and savings." },
  { term: "Number of lights", meaning: "How many light points a meter feeds, set on its Edit form. Reports use it for Energy per light: kWh per light a day." },
  { term: "Lights-on hours", meaning: "The hours lighting should run, set in Facility → Operating hours instead of opening hours. Use outside them counts as daytime use. A light that comes on or goes off within 20 minutes of them counts as on time." },
  { term: "Average day", meaning: "What the site, or a group, uses on a typical day in the chosen dates, from days with all their readings. Shown as “Uses about … a day” on the Overview, in Analysis and in reports." },
  { term: "No use (idle meter)", meaning: "A meter that is online but used nothing, so its count did not move and it sent no new readings. Normal for something rarely used, such as a fan. Shown as No use in this period or No use since …" },
  { term: "Stopped sending", meaning: "A meter that has sent nothing for half a day or more and is not just idle: it is offline, or it is a light that should come on every night or stay on. The Overview, the bell and an alert email name it. Check it on site." },
  { term: "Used while offline", meaning: "Energy a meter kept counting while offline and sent when it came back. The amount is real; only when in those hours it was used is estimated. Shown in Data availability." },
  { term: "Usual day", meaning: "The middle value of the complete days in the chosen dates; it needs at least five. A day stands out when it differs from this by more than 3% and by more than twice the normal day-to-day variation." },
  { term: "Planned closure", meaning: "A day the site is closed on purpose, such as a company shutdown." },
  { term: "Special hours", meaning: "A day with opening hours different from the usual week." },
  { term: "Saving a change", meaning: "Changes you save in Facility apply across the app straight away. If one cannot go live, the Facility page says why and offers Make live now." },
  { term: "Yearly estimate", meaning: "The average day in the period multiplied by 365. A guide, not a forecast." },
  { term: "Savings range", meaning: "The lower figure assumes equipment only drops to its quietest nights; the higher figure assumes it is switched off whenever the site is closed." },
];

export const GUIDE_QUESTIONS: GuideQuestion[] = [
  { question: "Is the data live?", answer: "No. Readings are collected once a day for the previous full day, so pages show the newest complete days. Reload a page to pick up new data." },
  { question: "Why do some days look empty or low?", answer: "Some devices sent no readings, or only some, on those days. They are marked “Readings missing” or with a dashed border, and totals for them may be low." },
  { question: "Why don't the devices add up to the site total?", answer: "Board totals overlap with the devices on them, and some energy has no separate meter. “Where the energy goes” on Devices splits it so the list adds up to the total." },
  { question: "I changed a setting but nothing moved.", answer: "Changes you save in Facility apply straight away, so reload the page you are looking at. If the Facility page says some changes are not live yet, it shows why; choose Make live now to try again." },
  { question: "Do the days before a site was connected count as missing?", answer: "No. A new site is counted from its first reading. Hours when a meter reported no use, such as a light that is off by day, count as received. A meter marked Not in use does not stop a day counting as complete in reports." },
  { question: "Why does a meter say No use instead of Stopped sending?", answer: "A meter sends a reading only when its count moves. One that is online but using nothing, such as a fan rarely switched on, shows No use in this period: nothing to fix. A meter that is offline, or a light that should come on every night or stay on, shows Stopped sending: check it on site." },
  { question: "Why isn't a light that came on a few minutes late counted as daytime use?", answer: "A light that comes on or goes off within 20 minutes of the lights-on hours counts as on time. Beyond that, only what it used outside its allowed minutes counts. Analysis step 3's hour-by-hour bar counts every hour outside the lights-on hours, so its share can be higher; the page says so when it is." },
  { question: "Why do Analysis and the report show the same numbers?", answer: "On purpose. For the dates you choose, Analysis builds the same site report and follows its order, so the average day, the groups and the days that stood out match the report for those dates." },
  { question: "Why don't I see the daily report on the Overview?", answer: "The Overview shows the weekly or monthly report, so one day's ups and downs do not replace it. Daily reports are kept in Reports, and the bell says when the newest one is ready." },
  { question: "Why does the Portfolio open on a different period?", answer: "It opens on last month. If no site has readings for last month yet, for example sites that started this month, it opens on the latest period that has them and says so. You can still pick any period at the top." },
  { question: "Who can change settings?", answer: "Administrators can edit rates, hours, holidays, groups and the floor plan. Everyone else can view every page." },
  { question: "Can I switch to dark mode?", answer: "Yes. Use the sun and moon switch in the top bar. Your choice is remembered on this browser." },
];

